## 4. Caching and Checkpointing: Upstream vs Beellama Fork

The beellama fork diverges from upstream llama.cpp in two distinct caching subsystems: **in-memory prompt caching** (cross-request KV reuse) and **context checkpoints** (intra-slot KV snapshots for context-shift recovery). The prompt cache is largely inherited, but checkpointing behavior has been substantially rewritten.

### 4a. In-Memory Prompt Cache (Mostly Shared)

The `server_prompt_cache` (`tools/server/server-task.cpp`) is functionally identical between upstream and the fork:

- Cache entries are **consumed** on hit (`states.erase(it_best)`), not referenced — single-use KV reuse
- LCP-based matching via `get_common_prefix()` with `f_keep_best` ratio scoring
- LRU eviction under `--cache-ram` memory pressure with adaptive limit reduction on `bad_alloc`
- Checkpoints (`common_prompt_checkpoint`) are persisted alongside cached token vectors

No meaningful divergence here.

### 4b. Context Checkpoints: Major Behavioral Divergence

Context checkpoints are per-slot KV cache snapshots created during prompt processing, used for context-shift recovery when a slot approaches its context limit. The checkpoint creation logic in `pre_decode()` (`tools/server/server-context.cpp`) differs significantly:

#### Upstream: Span-Based Turn Boundary Detection

Upstream uses `message_spans` (a structured turn-span tracker) to detect user-message boundaries:

```cpp
// Upstream (reference/llama.cpp, tools/server/server-context.cpp:3463-3475)
const auto & spans = slot.task->params.message_spans;
const auto last_user_pos = spans.last_user_message_pos();

// ... inside the prompt batching loop ...
// stop the prompt batch exactly before a user message
if (spans.is_user_start(slot.prompt.n_tokens())) {
    break;
}
```

Checkpoint suppression after the loop:

```cpp
// Upstream (reference/llama.cpp, tools/server/server-context.cpp:3544-3548)
// skip ordinary mid-prompt checkpoints, unless the batch starts a user
// message or we are near the end of the prompt
if (!is_user_start && !near_prompt_end) {
    do_checkpoint = false;
}
```

Where `is_user_start = spans.is_user_start(n_tokens_start)` and `is_last_user_message = n_tokens_start == last_user_pos`.

The spacing policy exempts `is_last_user_message`:

```cpp
// Upstream (reference/llama.cpp, tools/server/server-context.cpp:3564)
do_checkpoint = do_checkpoint && (slot.prompt.checkpoints.empty() ||
    is_last_user_message ||
    n_tokens_start > slot.prompt.checkpoints.back().n_tokens + params_base.checkpoint_min_step);
```

#### Beellama Fork: Token-Count-Based `n_before_user`

The fork replaces the `message_spans` mechanism with a simpler `n_before_user` field on `task_params`:

```cpp
// Fork (llama.cpp, tools/server/server-task.h:66)
int32_t n_before_user = -1;
```

```cpp
// Fork (llama.cpp, tools/server/server-context.cpp:3135-3136)
const int32_t n_before_user = slot.task->params.n_before_user;
const bool n_before_user_known = n_before_user > 0;
```

The batch break at user boundaries is gated on checkpointing being enabled:

```cpp
// Fork (llama.cpp, tools/server/server-context.cpp:3167-3170)
// stop the prompt batch exactly before the latest user input, so a checkpoint
// can be created after the previous messages
if (n_before_user_known &&
    slot.prompt.n_tokens() == n_before_user) {
    break;
}
```

Checkpoint suppression uses a three-tier `is_allowed` gate instead of the upstream's simple `is_user_start || near_prompt_end` check:

```cpp
// Fork (llama.cpp, tools/server/server-context.cpp:3215-3217)
// skip ordinary mid-prompt checkpoints
if (!n_before_user_known && !near_prompt_end) {
    do_checkpoint = false;
}
```

```cpp
// Fork (llama.cpp, tools/server/server-context.cpp:3227-3243)
const bool is_on_user =
    n_before_user_known &&
    n_tokens_start == n_before_user;

const bool is_after_user =
    n_before_user_known &&
    n_tokens_start > n_before_user;

const bool is_allowed =
    !n_before_user_known ||
    is_on_user ||
    (is_after_user && near_prompt_end);

if (do_checkpoint && !is_allowed) {
    do_checkpoint = false;
}
```

This means: **when `n_before_user` is known, checkpoints are only created at exactly two positions** — the token position matching `n_before_user` itself (`is_on_user`), or positions after the user message that are also near the prompt end (`is_after_user && near_prompt_end`). Mid-prompt checkpoints between user turns are suppressed.

#### Key Behavioral Differences

| Aspect | Upstream | Beellama Fork |
|--------|----------|---------------|
| Turn-boundary detection | `spans.is_user_start()` (structured span tracker) | `n_before_user` token count (single integer) |
| Batch break at user start | Unconditional (`if (spans.is_user_start(...)) break`) | Gated on `do_checkpoint && n_before_user_known` |
| Checkpoint suppression | `!is_user_start && !near_prompt_end` | `is_allowed` gate with `is_on_user` / `is_after_user` / `near_prompt_end` |
| Spacing exemption | `is_last_user_message` bypasses `checkpoint_min_step` | `near_prompt_end` bypasses `checkpoint_min_step` (no `is_last_user_message` exemption) |
| Checkpoint density | More checkpoints at user-start boundaries | Fewer checkpoints when `n_before_user` is known — restricted to two positions |

#### Implications

- **When `n_before_user` is known** (i.e., the server can determine where the last user turn ends), the fork creates **fewer checkpoints** than upstream, concentrating them at the user-turn boundary and near-prompt-end positions. This reduces checkpoint memory overhead but may increase re-decoding cost during context shifts if the optimal restore point falls between the two allowed positions.

- **When `n_before_user` is unknown** (`n_before_user == -1`), the fork falls back to `near_prompt_end` as the sole checkpoint creation gate — similar to upstream but without the `is_user_start` exemption, potentially creating fewer mid-prompt checkpoints.

- The `near_prompt_end` override in the spacing policy is **new in the fork** — upstream only exempts `is_last_user_message`. This means the fork is more aggressive about creating checkpoints near prompt ends regardless of turn structure.

- The `n_before_user` value is populated by `prompt_get_n_before_user()` during task construction (`tools/server/server-context.cpp:3742-3852`), which analyzes `message_spans` to compute the token position before the last user message — so the fork still uses spans internally, but compresses the result into a single integer for simpler checkpoint gating.

- `tools/server/server-task.h:66` — `n_before_user` field definition
- `tools/server/server-context.cpp:3135-3136` — `n_before_user_known` gate
- `tools/server/server-context.cpp:3167-3170` — checkpoint-aware batch break
- `tools/server/server-context.cpp:3215-3243` — `is_allowed` checkpoint suppression gate
- `tools/server/server-context.cpp:3742-3852` — `prompt_get_n_before_user()` computation
- `tools/server/server-context.cpp:3255` — spacing policy with `near_prompt_end` override
SECTION_END
```

